BODY_BEGIN
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000001 "=head1 NAME"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000002 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000003 "Types::Serialiser - simple data types for common serialisation formats"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000004 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000005 "=encoding utf-8"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000006 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000007 "=head1 SYNOPSIS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000008 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000009 "=head1 DESCRIPTION"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000010 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000011 "This module provides some extra datatypes that are used by common"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000012 "serialisation formats such as JSON or CBOR. The idea is to have a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000013 "repository of simple/small constants and containers that can be shared by"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000014 "different implementations so they become interoperable between each other."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000015 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000016 "=cut"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000017 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000018 "package Types::Serialiser;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000019 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000020 "use common::sense; # required to suppress annoying warnings"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000021 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000022 "our $VERSION = '1.01';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000023 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000024 "=head1 SIMPLE SCALAR CONSTANTS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000025 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000026 "Simple scalar constants are values that are overloaded to act like simple"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000027 "Perl values, but have (class) type to differentiate them from normal Perl"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000028 "scalars. This is necessary because these have different representations in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000029 "the serialisation formats."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000030 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000031 "In the following, functions with zero or one arguments have a prototype of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000032 "C<()> and C<($)>, respectively, so act as constants and unary operators."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000033 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000034 "=head2 BOOLEANS (Types::Serialiser::Boolean class)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000035 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000036 "This type has only two instances, true and false. A natural representation"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000037 "for these in Perl is C<1> and C<0>, but serialisation formats need to be"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000038 "able to differentiate between them and mere numbers."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000039 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000040 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000041 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000042 "=item $Types::Serialiser::true, Types::Serialiser::true"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000043 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000044 "This value represents the \"true\" value. In most contexts is acts like"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000045 "the number C<1>. It is up to you whether you use the variable form"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000046 "(C<$Types::Serialiser::true>) or the constant form (C<Types::Serialiser::true>)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000047 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000048 "The constant is represented as a reference to a scalar containing C<1> -"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000049 "implementations are allowed to directly test for this."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000050 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000051 "=item $Types::Serialiser::false, Types::Serialiser::false"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000052 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000053 "This value represents the \"false\" value. In most contexts is acts like"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000054 "the number C<0>. It is up to you whether you use the variable form"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000055 "(C<$Types::Serialiser::false>) or the constant form (C<Types::Serialiser::false>)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000056 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000057 "The constant is represented as a reference to a scalar containing C<0> -"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000058 "implementations are allowed to directly test for this."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000059 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000060 "=item Types::Serialiser::as_bool $value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000061 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000062 "Converts a Perl scalar into a boolean, which is useful syntactic"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000063 "sugar. Strictly equivalent to:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000064 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000065 "   $value ? $Types::Serialiser::true : $Types::Serialiser::false"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000066 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000067 "=item $is_bool = Types::Serialiser::is_bool $value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000068 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000069 "Returns true iff the C<$value> is either C<$Types::Serialiser::true> or"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000070 "C<$Types::Serialiser::false>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000071 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000072 "For example, you could differentiate between a perl true value and a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000073 "C<Types::Serialiser::true> by using this:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000074 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000075 "   $value && Types::Serialiser::is_bool $value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000076 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000077 "=item $is_true = Types::Serialiser::is_true $value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000078 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000079 "Returns true iff C<$value> is C<$Types::Serialiser::true>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000080 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000081 "=item $is_false = Types::Serialiser::is_false $value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000082 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000083 "Returns false iff C<$value> is C<$Types::Serialiser::false>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000084 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000085 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000086 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000087 "=head2 ERROR (Types::Serialiser::Error class)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000088 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000089 "This class has only a single instance, C<error>. It is used to signal"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000090 "an encoding or decoding error. In CBOR for example, and object that"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000091 "couldn't be encoded will be represented by a CBOR undefined value, which"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000092 "is represented by the error value in Perl."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000093 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000094 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000095 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000096 "=item $Types::Serialiser::error, Types::Serialiser::error"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000097 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000098 "This value represents the \"error\" value. Accessing values of this type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000099 "will throw an exception."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000100 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000101 "The constant is represented as a reference to a scalar containing C<undef>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000102 "- implementations are allowed to directly test for this."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000103 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000104 "=item $is_error = Types::Serialiser::is_error $value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000105 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000106 "Returns false iff C<$value> is C<$Types::Serialiser::error>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000107 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000108 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000109 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000110 "=cut"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000111 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000112 "BEGIN {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000113 "   # for historical reasons, and to avoid extra dependencies in JSON::PP,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000114 "   # we alias *Types::Serialiser::Boolean with JSON::PP::Boolean."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000115 "   package JSON::PP::Boolean;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000116 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000117 "   *Types::Serialiser::Boolean:: = *JSON::PP::Boolean::;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000118 "}"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000119 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000120 "{"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000121 "   # this must done before blessing to work around bugs"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000122 "   # in perl < 5.18 (it seems to be fixed in 5.18)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000123 "   package Types::Serialiser::BooleanBase;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000124 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000125 "   use overload"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000126 "      \"0+\"     => sub { ${$_[0]} },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000127 "      \"++\"     => sub { $_[0] = ${$_[0]} + 1 },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000128 "      \"--\"     => sub { $_[0] = ${$_[0]} - 1 },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000129 "      fallback => 1;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000130 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000131 "   @Types::Serialiser::Boolean::ISA = Types::Serialiser::BooleanBase::;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000132 "}"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000133 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000134 "our $true  = do { bless \\(my $dummy = 1), Types::Serialiser::Boolean:: };"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000135 "our $false = do { bless \\(my $dummy = 0), Types::Serialiser::Boolean:: };"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000136 "our $error = do { bless \\(my $dummy    ), Types::Serialiser::Error::   };"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000137 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000138 "sub true  () { $true  }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000139 "sub false () { $false }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000140 "sub error () { $error }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000141 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000142 "sub as_bool($) { $_[0] ? $true : $false }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000143 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000144 "sub is_bool  ($) {           UNIVERSAL::isa $_[0], Types::Serialiser::Boolean:: }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000145 "sub is_true  ($) {  $_[0] && UNIVERSAL::isa $_[0], Types::Serialiser::Boolean:: }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000146 "sub is_false ($) { !$_[0] && UNIVERSAL::isa $_[0], Types::Serialiser::Boolean:: }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000147 "sub is_error ($) {           UNIVERSAL::isa $_[0], Types::Serialiser::Error::   }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000148 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000149 "package Types::Serialiser::Error;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000150 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000151 "sub error {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000152 "   require Carp;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000153 "   Carp::croak (\"caught attempt to use the Types::Serialiser::error value\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000154 "};"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000155 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000156 "use overload"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000157 "   \"0+\"     => \\&error,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000158 "   \"++\"     => \\&error,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000159 "   \"--\"     => \\&error,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000160 "   fallback => 1;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000161 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000162 "=head1 NOTES FOR XS USERS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000163 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000164 "The recommended way to detect whether a scalar is one of these objects"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000165 "is to check whether the stash is the C<Types::Serialiser::Boolean> or"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000166 "C<Types::Serialiser::Error> stash, and then follow the scalar reference to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000167 "see if it's C<1> (true), C<0> (false) or C<undef> (error)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000168 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000169 "While it is possible to use an isa test, directly comparing stash pointers"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000170 "is faster and guaranteed to work."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000171 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000172 "For historical reasons, the C<Types::Serialiser::Boolean> stash is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000173 "just an alias for C<JSON::PP::Boolean>. When printed, the classname"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000174 "with usually be C<JSON::PP::Boolean>, but isa tests and stash pointer"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000175 "comparison will normally work correctly (i.e. Types::Serialiser::true ISA"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000176 "JSON::PP::Boolean, but also ISA Types::Serialiser::Boolean)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000177 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000178 "=head1 A GENERIC OBJECT SERIALIATION PROTOCOL"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000179 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000180 "This section explains the object serialisation protocol used by"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000181 "L<CBOR::XS>. It is meant to be generic enough to support any kind of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000182 "generic object serialiser."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000183 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000184 "This protocol is called \"the Types::Serialiser object serialisation"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000185 "protocol\"."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000186 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000187 "=head2 ENCODING"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000188 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000189 "When the encoder encounters an object that it cannot otherwise encode (for"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000190 "example, L<CBOR::XS> can encode a few special types itself, and will first"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000191 "attempt to use the special C<TO_CBOR> serialisation protocol), it will"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000192 "look up the C<FREEZE> method on the object."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000193 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000194 "Note that the C<FREEZE> method will normally be called I<during> encoding,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000195 "and I<MUST NOT> change the data structure that is being encoded in any"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000196 "way, or it might cause memory corruption or worse."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000197 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000198 "If it exists, it will call it with two arguments: the object to serialise,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000199 "and a constant string that indicates the name of the data model. For"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000200 "example L<CBOR::XS> uses C<CBOR>, and the L<JSON> and L<JSON::XS> modules"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000201 "(or any other JSON serialiser), would use C<JSON> as second argument."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000202 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000203 "The C<FREEZE> method can then return zero or more values to identify the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000204 "object instance. The serialiser is then supposed to encode the class name"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000205 "and all of these return values (which must be encodable in the format)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000206 "using the relevant form for Perl objects. In CBOR for example, there is a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000207 "registered tag number for encoded perl objects."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000208 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000209 "The values that C<FREEZE> returns must be serialisable with the serialiser"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000210 "that calls it. Therefore, it is recommended to use simple types such as"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000211 "strings and numbers, and maybe array references and hashes (basically, the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000212 "JSON data model). You can always use a more complex format for a specific"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000213 "data model by checking the second argument, the data model."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000214 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000215 "The \"data model\" is not the same as the \"data format\" - the data model"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000216 "indicates what types and kinds of return values can be returned from"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000217 "C<FREEZE>. For example, in C<CBOR> it is permissible to return tagged CBOR"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000218 "values, while JSON does not support these at all, so C<JSON> would be a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000219 "valid (but too limited) data model name for C<CBOR::XS>. similarly, a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000220 "serialising format that supports more or less the same data model as JSON"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000221 "could use C<JSON> as data model without losing anything."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000222 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000223 "=head2 DECODING"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000224 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000225 "When the decoder then encounters such an encoded perl object, it should"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000226 "look up the C<THAW> method on the stored classname, and invoke it with the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000227 "classname, the constant string to identify the data model/data format, and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000228 "all the return values returned by C<FREEZE>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000229 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000230 "=head2 EXAMPLES"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000231 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000232 "See the C<OBJECT SERIALISATION> section in the L<CBOR::XS> manpage for"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000233 "more details, an example implementation, and code examples."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000234 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000235 "Here is an example C<FREEZE>/C<THAW> method pair:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000236 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000237 "   sub My::Object::FREEZE {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000238 "      my ($self, $model) = @_;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000239 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000240 "      ($self->{type}, $self->{id}, $self->{variant})"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000241 "   }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000242 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000243 "   sub My::Object::THAW {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000244 "      my ($class, $model, $type, $id, $variant) = @_;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000245 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000246 "      $class->new (type => $type, id => $id, variant => $variant)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000247 "   }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000248 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000249 "=head1 BUGS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000250 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000251 "The use of L<overload> makes this module much heavier than it should be"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000252 "(on my system, this module: 4kB RSS, overload: 260kB RSS)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000253 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000254 "=head1 SEE ALSO"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000255 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000256 "Currently, L<JSON::XS> and L<CBOR::XS> use these types."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000257 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000258 "=head1 AUTHOR"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000259 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000260 " Marc Lehmann <schmorp@schmorp.de>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000261 " http://home.schmorp.de/"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000262 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000263 "=cut"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000264 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000265 "1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000266 ""
BODY_END
